1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,293
A company processes six
thousand invoices every month.

2
00:00:03,293 --> 00:00:05,763
A vendor offers
an AI agent

3
00:00:05,763 --> 00:00:08,645
and promises to automate
the entire queue.

4
00:00:08,645 --> 00:00:12,350
The finance team calculates
eighteen thousand dollars in monthly

5
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:16,466
labour cost and starts treating
the full amount as savings.

6
00:00:16,466 --> 00:00:20,037
There is one problem.
Processing a document is not

7
00:00:20,037 --> 00:00:23,609
the same as eliminating
the cost of processing it.

8
00:00:23,609 --> 00:00:25,593
Some invoices
still need people.

9
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:29,164
Every invoice needs controls.
The agent has operating costs.

10
00:00:29,164 --> 00:00:32,367
And the time released
from a task does not

11
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:35,570
automatically turn into cash.
In the next few minutes,

12
00:00:35,570 --> 00:00:38,773
we will calculate the actual
economics, compare three outcomes,

13
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:42,332
and build a decision rule
a finance leader can audit.

14
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:44,704
Every figure in this
example is hypothetical.

15
00:00:44,704 --> 00:00:46,398
The method is
the lesson.

16
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:49,066
Start with the work,
not the software.

17
00:00:49,066 --> 00:00:53,640
Six thousand invoices per month, five
minutes of manual work per invoice.

18
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:57,071
That is thirty thousand
minutes, or five hundred hours.

19
00:00:57,071 --> 00:01:00,451
At a fully loaded labour
rate of thirty-six dollars

20
00:01:00,451 --> 00:01:03,830
per hour, the monthly baseline
is eighteen thousand dollars.

21
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:06,459
Multiply by twelve
and the annual baseline

22
00:01:06,459 --> 00:01:08,711
is two hundred sixteen
thousand dollars.

23
00:01:08,711 --> 00:01:12,091
This is the reference cost,
not the agent's benefit.

24
00:01:12,091 --> 00:01:14,674
Record the source
of each input.

25
00:01:14,674 --> 00:01:19,841
Does five minutes come from
a timed sample or a sales slide?

26
00:01:19,841 --> 00:01:24,147
Does thirty-six dollars include
benefits and supervision? Do the six

27
00:01:24,147 --> 00:01:27,161
thousand invoices include duplicates,
cancellations and exceptions?

28
00:01:27,161 --> 00:01:30,083
If your baseline is unreliable,
every ROI percentage

29
00:01:30,083 --> 00:01:33,004
built on top of it
is unreliable too.

30
00:01:33,004 --> 00:01:36,291
Put the baseline on screen
before you discuss automation.

31
00:01:36,291 --> 00:01:38,438
The vendor says ninety
percent accuracy.

32
00:01:38,438 --> 00:01:42,017
That is not enough information
to build a business case.

33
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:44,880
Ask what percentage
of invoices reaches a correct,

34
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:46,670
approved endpoint
without manual repair.

35
00:01:46,670 --> 00:01:49,175
We will call that
the straight-through rate.

36
00:01:49,175 --> 00:01:51,793
For our base case, assume
seventy-five percent.

37
00:01:51,793 --> 00:01:55,907
Four thousand five hundred invoices
finish without the full manual process.

38
00:01:55,907 --> 00:01:59,273
The other fifteen hundred still
require five minutes each.

39
00:01:59,273 --> 00:02:02,639
That is one hundred twenty-five
hours of exception handling.

40
00:02:02,639 --> 00:02:07,312
At thirty-six dollars per hour,
exception handling costs four thousand

41
00:02:07,312 --> 00:02:11,985
five hundred dollars a month.
Now add a separate control.

42
00:02:11,985 --> 00:02:15,256
Suppose every invoice, including
successful ones, receives

43
00:02:15,256 --> 00:02:18,994
forty-five seconds of human review
or sampling-equivalent oversight.

44
00:02:18,994 --> 00:02:22,590
Six thousand times forty-five
seconds equals seventy-five hours,

45
00:02:22,590 --> 00:02:25,287
or two thousand seven
hundred dollars.

46
00:02:25,287 --> 00:02:30,231
In a real deployment, design
the review model to match risk.

47
00:02:30,231 --> 00:02:35,175
Some workflows use targeted samples
rather than review of every item.

48
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:38,276
For this teaching example,
the control assumption

49
00:02:38,276 --> 00:02:41,377
stays constant so the arithmetic
stays transparent.

50
00:02:41,377 --> 00:02:46,549
We now have seven thousand two hundred
dollars in retained human work.

51
00:02:46,549 --> 00:02:50,427
Add eighteen hundred dollars
each month for model calls,

52
00:02:50,427 --> 00:02:54,306
hosting and integration. Add twenty-two
hundred dollars for maintenance,

53
00:02:54,306 --> 00:02:56,892
evaluation, exception-rule
changes and operational support.

54
00:02:56,892 --> 00:03:00,666
Add a one-thousand-dollar monthly
risk reserve for expected remediation

55
00:03:00,666 --> 00:03:04,439
and quality incidents. That
reserve is a planning assumption,

56
00:03:04,439 --> 00:03:07,374
not a claim about
industry incident rates.

57
00:03:07,374 --> 00:03:11,567
The post-automation monthly cost is
twelve thousand two hundred dollars.

58
00:03:11,567 --> 00:03:13,949
Subtract that from
the eighteen-thousand-dollar baseline.

59
00:03:13,949 --> 00:03:16,728
The model yields five
thousand eight hundred

60
00:03:16,728 --> 00:03:19,904
dollars in monthly economic
benefit, not eighteen thousand.

61
00:03:19,904 --> 00:03:23,080
That is about thirty-two
percent of the baseline.

62
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:25,859
It is still a potentially
worthwhile project.

63
00:03:25,859 --> 00:03:28,917
It is simply not
the result implied by

64
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:32,740
a slide showing six thousand
invoices multiplied by five minutes.

65
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:36,274
Now include the cost
of getting into production.

66
00:03:36,274 --> 00:03:39,367
Assume thirty thousand dollars
in one-time integration,

67
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:42,460
testing, process redesign,
training and launch expense.

68
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:45,994
Divide thirty thousand by
five thousand eight hundred.

69
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:49,576
Simple payback is about five
point two months, provided

70
00:03:49,576 --> 00:03:53,555
the monthly benefit is realized
and stable. Over twelve months,

71
00:03:53,555 --> 00:03:57,137
the recurring benefit totals sixty-nine
thousand six hundred dollars.

72
00:03:57,137 --> 00:04:00,316
After the initial thirty
thousand dollars, the first-year

73
00:04:00,316 --> 00:04:03,893
net economic benefit is thirty-nine
thousand six hundred dollars.

74
00:04:03,893 --> 00:04:07,072
Relative to the thirty-thousand-dollar
initial investment, that is

75
00:04:07,072 --> 00:04:11,046
a simple first-year net return
of one hundred thirty-two percent.

76
00:04:11,046 --> 00:04:14,326
Do not confuse this
with accounting profit,

77
00:04:14,326 --> 00:04:18,544
internal rate of return
or a discounted cash flow.

78
00:04:18,544 --> 00:04:22,293
A serious investment memo
would also model ramp-up,

79
00:04:22,293 --> 00:04:26,511
taxes, financing, depreciation where
relevant, and timing of savings.

80
00:04:26,511 --> 00:04:29,713
Here is the number that
changes the decision.

81
00:04:29,713 --> 00:04:33,315
If straight-through completion
falls to sixty percent, two thousand

82
00:04:33,315 --> 00:04:36,917
four hundred invoices need the original
five-minute manual process.

83
00:04:36,917 --> 00:04:40,519
That exception work costs seven
thousand two hundred dollars.

84
00:04:40,519 --> 00:04:45,578
Keep the same two thousand seven
hundred dollars in review and five

85
00:04:45,578 --> 00:04:49,372
thousand dollars in combined operating,
maintenance and reserve costs.

86
00:04:49,372 --> 00:04:53,588
Total post-automation cost rises to
fourteen thousand nine hundred dollars.

87
00:04:53,588 --> 00:04:57,744
Savings fall to three thousand
one hundred dollars a month.

88
00:04:57,744 --> 00:05:01,070
Payback stretches to about
nine point seven months.

89
00:05:01,070 --> 00:05:04,395
If completion reaches ninety
percent, only six hundred

90
00:05:04,395 --> 00:05:07,720
invoices need manual processing,
costing eighteen hundred dollars.

91
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:09,814
With the other
costs unchanged,

92
00:05:09,814 --> 00:05:13,163
the total becomes nine
thousand five hundred dollars.

93
00:05:13,163 --> 00:05:17,351
Savings rise to eight thousand
five hundred dollars a month.

94
00:05:17,351 --> 00:05:20,700
Payback drops to roughly
three point five months.

95
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,050
Same agent concept. Same baseline.
Different exception rate.

96
00:05:24,050 --> 00:05:26,558
A vendor's headline
model score does not

97
00:05:26,558 --> 00:05:29,424
replace measured straight-through
results in your own workflow.

98
00:05:29,424 --> 00:05:33,498
A responsible AI business
case has three scorecards.

99
00:05:33,498 --> 00:05:37,063
First, quality: correct outputs,
policy exceptions, rework,

100
00:05:37,063 --> 00:05:41,647
incident severity and the time
needed to resolve errors.

101
00:05:41,647 --> 00:05:45,212
Second, economics: baseline
cost, retained human effort,

102
00:05:45,212 --> 00:05:49,286
operating cost, implementation
cost and the net benefit.

103
00:05:49,286 --> 00:05:53,587
Third, governance: who authorizes
actions, which data the agent accesses,

104
00:05:53,587 --> 00:05:57,459
whether every consequential
action is logged, and who stops

105
00:05:57,459 --> 00:06:01,330
the workflow when controls fail.
These measures belong together.

106
00:06:01,330 --> 00:06:04,742
An agent with high
throughput but poor

107
00:06:04,742 --> 00:06:07,666
auditability is not
a successful deployment.

108
00:06:07,666 --> 00:06:12,539
The United States National Institute
of Standards and Technology provides

109
00:06:12,539 --> 00:06:17,413
a voluntary AI risk-management
framework and a generative AI profile.

110
00:06:17,413 --> 00:06:21,355
OWASP's 2026 agentic-security
guidance identifies security risks in

111
00:06:21,355 --> 00:06:24,805
agents that plan
and act through tools.

112
00:06:24,805 --> 00:06:27,269
These are useful
reference points,

113
00:06:27,269 --> 00:06:31,211
not proof that any individual
product is compliant.

114
00:06:31,211 --> 00:06:34,355
Now the accounting distinction
most ROI videos miss.

115
00:06:34,355 --> 00:06:37,891
If the team saves hours
but salaries and contractor

116
00:06:37,891 --> 00:06:41,035
bills remain unchanged,
the company has released capacity.

117
00:06:41,035 --> 00:06:43,786
It has not automatically
reduced cash spending.

118
00:06:43,786 --> 00:06:47,312
Released capacity still
has value if staff

119
00:06:47,312 --> 00:06:50,838
handle more invoices, reduce
backlog, improve service

120
00:06:50,838 --> 00:06:54,867
or avoid future hiring. Record
those outcomes separately.

121
00:06:54,867 --> 00:06:59,296
Cash savings need evidence such
as lower overtime, fewer external

122
00:06:59,296 --> 00:07:03,282
processing invoices or a documented
change in staffing expenditure.

123
00:07:03,282 --> 00:07:06,382
Do not count
the same hour twice

124
00:07:06,382 --> 00:07:09,925
as both a cash reduction
and productivity gain.

125
00:07:09,925 --> 00:07:12,196
Ask finance
to sign off on

126
00:07:12,196 --> 00:07:14,846
the value category
before the pilot starts.

127
00:07:14,846 --> 00:07:18,632
Ask operations to sign off
on the actual exception rate.

128
00:07:18,632 --> 00:07:22,796
Ask risk and compliance to sign
off on the control design.

129
00:07:22,796 --> 00:07:26,030
And ask procurement
to test the exit path,

130
00:07:26,030 --> 00:07:28,455
including data export
and vendor dependency.

131
00:07:28,455 --> 00:07:30,461
Here is
the decision rule.

132
00:07:30,461 --> 00:07:33,268
Run the agent on
a representative sample.

133
00:07:33,268 --> 00:07:36,878
Measure the percentage of transactions
completed correctly without repair.

134
00:07:36,878 --> 00:07:40,488
Time every human exception. Record
review and maintenance effort.

135
00:07:40,488 --> 00:07:42,895
Recalculate the model
with real costs.

136
00:07:42,895 --> 00:07:47,045
If the benefit survives the low-case
scenario, the controls work,

137
00:07:47,045 --> 00:07:51,611
and a named owner accepts
the residual risk, scale in stages.

138
00:07:51,611 --> 00:07:54,516
If the economics
only work when every

139
00:07:54,516 --> 00:07:57,421
document is perfect,
keep the pilot small.

140
00:07:57,421 --> 00:08:01,864
In this hypothetical example,
the honest base case is five

141
00:08:01,864 --> 00:08:06,307
thousand eight hundred dollars
a month, not eighteen thousand. Follow

142
00:08:06,307 --> 00:08:10,305
PrismBay AI for practical business
systems with measurable outcomes.

143
00:08:10,305 --> 00:08:13,404
The model and assumptions
belong in your decision memo,

144
00:08:13,404 --> 00:08:15,470
not buried in
a vendor demonstration.
